# Inspect Activations

## Overview

A running Agent Server answers read requests while it works. You can read the
committed Agent, the Server's phase and queue, recent Turn outcomes, Plugin
state, and the processes in its activation. This Livebook starts one Agent
with a Plugin and two nested members, then reads each view.

<!-- livebook:{"force_markdown":true} -->

```elixir
Jido.AgentServer.agent(server)        # committed Agent
Jido.AgentServer.snapshot(server)     # Agent plus revision, read together
Jido.AgentServer.status(server)       # phase, queue, active Turn, last outcome
Jido.AgentServer.history(server)      # recent outcomes, with debug: true
```

Run the cells from top to bottom.

## Install

```elixir
Mix.install([{:jido, "~> 3.0.0-beta.2"}])
```

## The Example Agent

`Guides.Tally` is a Plugin that counts Turns in its own `:tally` field.
`Guides.Notes` uses it, declares two `Guides.Helper` members, and appends
text to `notes`. The `ms` field adds a delay.

```elixir
defmodule Guides.Tally do
  use Jido.Plugin

  @impl true
  def state_spec(_opts) do
    {:tally, Zoi.object(%{turns: Zoi.integer()}) |> Zoi.default(%{turns: 0})}
  end

  @impl true
  def reduce(%Jido.Agent.Plugin.Reduction{plugin_state: tally}, _opts) do
    {:ok, %{tally | turns: tally.turns + 1}}
  end
end

defmodule Guides.Helper do
  use Jido.Agent, name: "helper"

  state do
    schema(Zoi.object(%{jobs: Zoi.integer() |> Zoi.default(0)}))
  end
end

defmodule Guides.Notes do
  use Jido.Agent, name: "notes", extensions: [Guides.Tally]

  state do
    schema(Zoi.object(%{notes: Zoi.list(Zoi.string()) |> Zoi.default([])}))
  end

  topology do
    agent :helpers, Guides.Helper, count: 2
  end

  routes do
    signal_source("/guides/inspect")

    route "note.add", as: :add do
      action %{text: text, ms: ms},
        name: "add_note",
        schema: Zoi.object(%{text: Zoi.string(), ms: Zoi.integer() |> Zoi.default(0)}), context: context do
        Process.sleep(ms)
        {:ok, %{context.agent_state | notes: context.agent_state.notes ++ [text]}}
      end
    end
  end
end
```

## Start With Debug

`debug: true` keeps recent Turn outcomes for `history/1`.
`debug_max_events:` sets how many, default 100, range 1 to 10,000.

```elixir
{:ok, _} = Jido.start()
{:ok, server} = Jido.start_agent(Guides.Notes, id: "notes-1", debug: true, debug_max_events: 20)
Jido.AgentServer.await_ready(server)
```

The result is `:ok`. `start_agent/2` can return before Plugin runtimes and
nested members are ready. `await_ready/2` waits for them. Until then, new
Turns return `{:error, :not_ready}`. A caller timeout on `await_ready/2`
doesn't stop startup.

## Read the Agent

```elixir
note = Guides.Notes.add_signal!(%{text: "first"})
{:ok, _agent, []} = Jido.AgentServer.call(server, note)

Jido.AgentServer.agent(server).state
```

The result is `%{notes: ["first"], tally: %{turns: 1}}`. `agent/2` returns the
committed Agent. It never shows a candidate from a running Turn.

`plugin_state/3` reads one Plugin's field:

```elixir
{Jido.AgentServer.plugin_state(server, Guides.Tally),
 Jido.AgentServer.plugin_state(server, Enum)}
```

The result is `{{:ok, %{turns: 1}}, {:error, :plugin_not_found}}`.

`snapshot/2` returns the Agent and its revision from the same Server event:

```elixir
snapshot = Jido.AgentServer.snapshot(server)
{snapshot.state_version, snapshot.agent.state.notes}
```

The result is `{1, ["first"]}`. Two separate reads, `agent/2` then
`status/2`, can straddle a commit. Use `snapshot/2` when the revision must
match the state.

## Read the Status

`status/2` returns a map. Read the fields you need:

```elixir
status = Jido.AgentServer.status(server)
Map.take(status, [:phase, :ready?, :agent_id, :state_version, :error_count, :admission])
```

The result is:

```text
%{
  phase: :idle,
  ready?: true,
  agent_id: "notes-1",
  state_version: 1,
  error_count: 0,
  admission: %{mode: :open, postponed: 0, limit: 1000, message_queue_len: 0}
}
```

| Field | Value |
| --- | --- |
| `phase` | `:initializing`, `:idle`, `:running`, or `:waiting` (a durable Flow is suspended). |
| `ready?` | Plugin runtimes and members are ready. |
| `agent_id`, `partition`, `jido` | Identity. |
| `state_version` | Committed revision. Increases by 1 per commit. |
| `generation` | A reference that changes each time the Server process starts, recovery included. |
| `activation` | The activation supervisor PID. |
| `parent` | The parent link for a nested member, or `nil`. |
| `plugin_count` | Attached Plugins. |
| `admission` | Mode (`:open` or `:quiesced`), queued Turns, limit, mailbox length. |
| `active` | The running Turn, or `nil`. |
| `last_outcome` | The most recent finished Turn. |
| `effects`, `last_effect_outcome` | The running and the last Directive batch. |
| `error_count` | Consecutive failed Turns, for `{:max_errors, n}`. |
| `owners`, `idle_timeout`, `idle_policy` | Idle shutdown. See [Supervision and Lifecycle](agent-activation-control.md). |
| `durable_turn`, `waits` | Durable Flow progress. |

`last_outcome` is a Turn summary:

```elixir
Map.take(status.last_outcome, [:status, :stage, :committed?, :state_version_before, :state_version_after])
```

The result is
`%{status: :succeeded, stage: :commit, committed?: true, state_version_before: 0, state_version_after: 1}`.
The full summary also has `signal_id`, `turn_id`, `directive_count`, and
`duration_ms`. `status` is `:succeeded`, `:failed`, `:cancelled`, or
`:timed_out`. Summaries never contain Agent state, Signal data, or context.

## See the Active Turn

Submit a slow Turn and read `active` while it runs:

```elixir
slow = Guides.Notes.add_signal!(%{text: "slow", ms: 300})
pending = Jido.AgentServer.submit(server, slow)

active =
  Enum.find_value(1..100, fn _ ->
    Process.sleep(10)
    Jido.AgentServer.status(server).active
  end)

{:ok, _agent, []} = Jido.AgentServer.await(pending, 2_000)
{Jido.AgentServer.status(server).phase, active.stage, active.signal_id == slow.id}
```

The result is `{:idle, :running, true}`. `active` also has `turn_id`,
`start_version`, `committed_version`, and `worker_pid`. Pass `turn_id` to
`cancel_turn/3`; see [Admission and Deadlines](agent-server-admission.md).

## Read the History

`history/1` returns retained outcomes, oldest first:

```elixir
server
|> Jido.AgentServer.history()
|> Enum.map(&{&1.status, &1.state_version_after})
```

The result is `[succeeded: 1, succeeded: 2]`. Without `debug: true`,
`history/1` returns `[]`. The history lives in Server memory and is lost when
the Server stops.

## The Activation Tree

Each started Agent has an activation: a supervisor that holds its Server and
its nested members.

```elixir
activation = Jido.AgentServer.activation(server)

activation
|> Supervisor.which_children()
|> Enum.map(fn {id, _pid, type, _modules} -> {id, type} end)
```

The result is `[{:owned_agents, :supervisor}, {Jido.AgentServer, :worker}]`. `activation/1` returns `nil` for a bare `start_link/1` worker.

`children/2` lists live children by tag:

```elixir
server |> Jido.AgentServer.children() |> Map.keys() |> Enum.sort()
```

The result is `["$topology/helpers/1", "$topology/helpers/2"]`. Each value is
`%{pid: pid, activation: pid, placement: :owned}`. `members/3` returns the
PIDs for one topology member, in slot order:

```elixir
{length(Jido.AgentServer.members(server, :helpers)), Jido.AgentServer.members(server, :missing)}
```

The result is `{2, []}`. See [Nested Agents](declare-agent-topology.livemd)
for topology.

## Owners and Idle Time

An owner is a process that keeps the Agent from idle shutdown:

```elixir
:ok = Jido.AgentServer.attach(server)
owners = Jido.AgentServer.status(server).owners
:ok = Jido.AgentServer.detach(server)

{owners == [self()], Jido.AgentServer.status(server).owners, Jido.AgentServer.touch(server)}
```

The result is `{true, [], :ok}`.

- `attach/3` adds an owner. A repeated attach is a no-op.
- `detach/3` removes one. The last detach starts a new idle period.
- `touch/1` restarts the idle period. It doesn't open closed admission.

They matter only with a finite `idle_timeout:`. See
[Supervision and Lifecycle](agent-activation-control.md).

## Stop the Example

```elixir
:ok = Jido.stop_agent(server)
:ok = Jido.stop()
```

Stopping the Agent stops its activation and both helpers.

